commonMistakes:
  - title: "Using Fast Hashes (SHA-256/MD5) for Passwords"
    description: "Fast cryptographic hash functions can compute billions of guesses per second on consumer GPUs. Passwords must always use slow, memory-hard key derivation functions like Argon2, bcrypt, or PBKDF2 with unique salts."
  - title: "Using SHA-1 or MD5 in New Systems"
    description: "SHA-1 and MD5 have proven practical collision vulnerabilities (e.g. SHAttered attack). They are obsolete and insecure for digital signatures, certificates, and integrity guarantees."
  - title: "Ignoring Character Encoding (UTF-8 vs Latin-1)"
    description: "Hashing depends on raw binary bytes. Hashing multi-byte Unicode characters (accents, emojis) in different encodings produces completely divergent digests."
  - title: "Relying on Unsalted Hashes"
    description: "Hashing without cryptographic salting allows attackers to reverse digests instantly using precomputed rainbow tables."
sources:
